You seem to be under the impression that either a) I'm complaining
about the deaths (I'm not, I made my peace with the capriciousness of
the RNG long ago) or b) I want the game to be easier (hell no).

We're talking about someone new enough that "The giant eel swings
itself around you!" doesn't give them pause.  I wouldn't imagine that
someone that new has bothered to scour the guidebook or use the
extended-description command liberally.

Nethack is, among other things, quite dependent on powers of
observation.  In all but the most brilliant of us (which I'm willing to
grant that you are, if you'd like), those powers of observation are
learned, not inherent.  I know I didn't read the guidebook before my
first (or fifteenth) game, and I very well may have fallen into a
poisonous pit and instantly died in that first (or fifteenth) game.

There're two of your rebuttals that I still take issue with, though:

> In general, the set things you can gainfully eat in nethack in
> considerably larger than the set of things real-life experience
> suggests it might be safe to eat. IOW, rather than asking "How would a
> n00b know to avoid eating slime?" it would be better to ask "How would
> a n00b know it's okay to eat fresh jackal corpses?"

In my case, I *believe* (it's been a long time) I discovered that I
could "eat corpses" by noticing that corpses looked just like food
rations.  So then I went out and ate everything I could find, and
slowly learned that blobs and puddings (which I still mentally group
together, which causes me problems with my monk games) tended to make
strange messages appear like "You feel healthy" and "Your health
currently feels amplified".  I later learned -- as I became spoiled,
rather than within the game -- that those messages meant I was gaining
resistances.  The first time I saw a green slime was in Juiblex's
Swamp, and I looked up whether it gave any resistances before passing
it by.  Only later did I learn (by reading the Instadeath spoiler) that
it would've actually killed me.

> > ...that standing next to a drawbridge while doing anything to it is
> > likely going to result in death?
>
> A huge, heavy piece of dark-ages machinery under the control of
> intelligent adversaries? Is it really that much of a stretch to
> imagine that's a good thing to not stand directly in front of?

One of the ones that still gets me (meaning "doesn't make sense to me",
rather than "kills my characters") is that a wand of striking zapped
from the outside of the castle somehow shatters the bridge in such a
way that it falls back towards the zapper.  When I throw a baseball
through a window, glass doesn't fly at me -- it goes with the ball.

Still, like I said before, at this point I'm fully-spoiled and just try
to avoid the instadeaths rather than worry too much about how
inconsistent they might be.

